<div class="about-container">

    <h1>About Bijaganita</h1>

    <p class="intro">
        <b>Bijaganita (बीजगणित)</b> is a traditional Indian mathematical
        discipline concerned with calculations involving unknown quantities,
        equations, rules, and mathematical relationships.
    </p>

    <h2>What Does Bijaganita Mean?</h2>

    <p>
        The Sanskrit term <b>Bijaganita</b> is commonly explained from
        <b>Bij</b>, meaning "seed", and <b>Ganita</b>, meaning
        "calculation" or "mathematics". The idea of a "seed" can be
        understood as an unknown quantity from which a calculation develops.
    </p>

    <p>
        In the history of Indian mathematics, Bijaganita became associated
        with methods for solving problems involving unknown quantities,
        equations, positive and negative numbers, and mathematical rules.
    </p>


    <h2>History of Bijaganita</h2>

    <p>
        Indian mathematicians developed sophisticated methods for working
        with numbers and unknown quantities. The mathematician
        <b>Bhaskara II</b>, also known as Bhaskaracharya, wrote a work called
        <b>Bijaganita</b> as part of his mathematical writings in the
        12th century.
    </p>

    <p>
        His work discussed mathematical operations, equations, unknown
        quantities, and methods for solving different types of problems.
        These ideas form an important part of the history of algebra in
        India.
    </p>


    <h2>How Are Mathematical Formulas Built?</h2>

    <p>
        A mathematical formula is a short way of representing a relationship
        between quantities. Formulas are developed by identifying the
        quantities involved and determining how they are related.
    </p>

    <p>
        For example, the area of a rectangle depends on its length and width.
        Therefore, its formula is:
    </p>

    <div class="formula-box">
        Area = Length × Width
    </div>

    <p>
        This tells us that if we know the length and width, we can calculate
        the area.
    </p>


    <h2>Examples of Formulas</h2>

    <div class="formula-card">

        <h3>Circle</h3>

        <p>
            The area of a circle depends on its radius.
        </p>

        <div class="formula-box">
            Area = π × r²
        </div>

        <p>
            <b>r</b> represents the radius or
            <b>त्रिज्या</b>.
        </p>

    </div>


    <div class="formula-card">

        <h3>Rectangle</h3>

        <p>
            The area of a rectangle is calculated using its length and width.
        </p>

        <div class="formula-box">
            Area = Length × Width
        </div>

        <p>
            Length = <b>लंबाई</b><br>
            Width = <b>चौड़ाई</b>
        </p>

    </div>


    <div class="formula-card">

        <h3>Triangle</h3>

        <p>
            The area of a triangle depends on its base and perpendicular
            height.
        </p>

        <div class="formula-box">
            Area = ½ × Base × Height
        </div>

        <p>
            Base = <b>आधार</b><br>
            Height = <b>ऊँचाई</b>
        </p>

    </div>


    <div class="formula-card">

        <h3>Square</h3>

        <p>
            A square has four equal sides, so its area can be calculated
            by multiplying one side by itself.
        </p>

        <div class="formula-box">
            Area = Side × Side
        </div>

        <p>
            Side = <b>भुजा</b>
        </p>

    </div>


    <h2>Mathematical Terms Used in Bijaganita</h2>

    <table class="terms-table">

        <tr>
            <th>English</th>
            <th>Hindi / Sanskrit Term</th>
            <th>Meaning</th>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Radius</td>
            <td>त्रिज्या</td>
            <td>Distance from the centre to the circle</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Diameter</td>
            <td>व्यास</td>
            <td>Distance across a circle through its centre</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Side</td>
            <td>भुजा</td>
            <td>A straight edge of a geometric shape</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Length</td>
            <td>लंबाई</td>
            <td>The length of a shape</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Width</td>
            <td>चौड़ाई</td>
            <td>The width of a shape</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Base</td>
            <td>आधार</td>
            <td>The reference side of a triangle</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Height</td>
            <td>ऊँचाई</td>
            <td>Perpendicular distance from the base</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Area</td>
            <td>क्षेत्रफल</td>
            <td>The space inside a shape</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Perimeter</td>
            <td>परिमाप</td>
            <td>The distance around a shape</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Centre</td>
            <td>केंद्र</td>
            <td>The central point of a shape</td>
        </tr>

    </table>


    <h2>How Bijaganita Helps Us Learn</h2>

    <p>
        Mathematics becomes easier when a formula is understood instead of
        simply memorised. Bijaganita encourages us to understand the
        quantities involved, the relationship between them, and the steps
        needed to solve a problem.
    </p>

    <p>
        This website follows the same learning approach by combining
        explanations, formulas, examples, mathematical terminology, and
        visual animations.
    </p>


    <h2>About This Website</h2>

    <p>
        <b>Bijaganita</b> is an educational mathematics website created to
        help students learn mathematical formulas in a simple and interactive
        way.
    </p>

    <p>
        Students can select a formula, read its explanation, see an example,
        learn the related mathematical terms, and use an animation to
        understand the geometric concept visually.
    </p>

